#
# prima-materia — the Finnic incantations, taken off the register
#
# Taken from traditions/registry-western-esoteric.ttl on the testability-driven
# entrance of SPEC 14. The registration stated the claim to be tested in one
# line: power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin. That
# claim is here, at the wording, in the one place where the songs state it of
# themselves rather than having it stated about them.
#
# What the reading found beside it is worth more. This transmission writes its
# own failure handling into the operative text. Where an expulsion has produced
# nothing, a further formula follows, and that formula is built as a ladder of
# six rungs, each introduced by a conditional on the last having produced
# nothing, ending not in a failure but in the sentence that the speaker still
# remembers other means. The same construction recurs inside an exorcism. A
# transmission that scripts what to say when nothing happened has put into its
# own text what this holding elsewhere has to read off a doctrine.
#

pmt:FinnicIncantation a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Finnic incantation"@en, "Finnische Zaubersprüche"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Finnish and Karelian transmission of the tietäjä, in which power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin."@en ;
    skos:note "Kalevala is a nineteenth-century compilation and belongs in the record as an ordering act, not as the corpus. The corpus is the collected incantations."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot; the loitsurunot collections" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    skos:note "Read in one work and at the wording of its English: John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II, The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), which translates 639 pieces of Elias Lönnrot's collection of 1880. Read in full: the editor's account of the eighteen classes of formula and of the order of recitation, pp. 2-6; § 3 Against Envy, pp. 70-72; § 5 To Discover the Cause, pp. 73-75; § 12 Falling into Ecstasy, pp. 83-84; § 16 Menacing, pp. 92-94; § 17 Exorcisms at a to h, pp. 94-99; § 210 a, p. 332. The whole volume searched by term for signs of success and for stated terms, and every hit read in its place."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition read is a translation of a compilation, and the difficulty is in the compilation and not in the translation. Lönnrot gives no place of recording and contaminates as many as twenty variants into one text, which the translator states in his own opening pages. A node in this file therefore names a formula type and not an aufzeichnung: it says that this is how the type runs in the standard collection, not that any one singer sang it so. The corpus that would carry the second claim is Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot, which prints the recordings singly with their place, collector and date, and it was not opened in this run because no public-domain English translation of it exists."@en ;
    skos:note "What was not read, and it is the larger part. Of the volume's 233 numbered sections, seven were read; the origins themselves, §§ 183-233, were read only at § 210. Volume I was not opened. Neither Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot nor Lönnrot's own edition was opened. The Kalevala was deliberately not used, for the reason the registration gives: it is an ordering act and not the corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays disputed and the reading moved it only by adding the translator's own judgement, which is recorded as his and not entered as a claim. He states that the origin or genealogy passage occurs about a hundred and fifty times in the songs and is also found in Estonian charms, which closely resemble the Finnish, but not elsewhere so far as he knows — and that a different structural element, the narrative precedent, is probably borrowed from the Latvians. That is a scholar comparing corpora in 1898 and belongs at a protocol or in an issue, not in a node of this file."@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II: The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332, translating Elias Lönnrot (ed.), Suomen Kansan Muinaisia Loitsurunoja (Helsinki: SKS, 1880)" .

#
# The claim the registration was made on
#

pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Pronouncing the origin of the thing"@en, "Das Aussprechen des Ursprungs der Sache"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "synty"@en, "synnyn sanat"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reciting, to the cause of a hurt, the account of how it came to be: its birth, its parentage and its descent, in the words that are handed down. The songs treat it as a thing the operator either has or lacks, and the collection carries a separate formula for the case in which he lacks it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332" .

pmc:FinnicPahanPoisto a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The needless evil is expelled"@en, "Das unnötige Übel wird ausgetrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the song states of the recitation, in one line and without a condition attached: by pronouncing a deep origin, words that are handed down, a needless evil is expelled."@en ;
    skos:note "The line is the whole of the transmission's own statement of its central doctrine, and it is worth noticing how little it says. It names no interval, no repetition, no sign, and no state of either party. Everything else this holding will carry about the synty comes from what the operator does when it does not work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332" .

pmc:FinnicSyntyYieldsExpulsion a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Pronouncing the origin expels the evil"@en, "Das Aussprechen des Ursprungs treibt das Übel aus"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:FinnicPahanPoisto ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332" .

pmp:FinnicSyynTiedustus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The formula said when the origin is not known"@en, "Die Formel, wenn der Ursprung nicht bekannt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The formula for the case in which the operator does not know what he is dealing with. Its speaker says outright that he does not know who the maker of the hurt is, and undertakes to get to know its origin and to ascertain its birthplace; it then puts the alternatives to the hurt itself, whether it is the Creator's work or a man's."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission whose doctrine is that knowing the origin gives power, and which carries a standing procedure for not knowing it, has admitted the ordinary case into its own apparatus. The formula is not an emergency measure: it has a number and a place in the order of recitation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 5 a-c, pp. 73-75" .

pmc:FinnicSyntyPresupposesKnowingIt a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The origin must be known before it can be pronounced"@en, "Der Ursprung muss gewusst sein, ehe er gesprochen werden kann"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FinnicSyynTiedustus ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The collection places the discovery formula before the origins in its order of recitation and says it is said in order to discover the origin; nothing read states what befalls the operator who recites an origin he has not got right. The strength is set at what the wording carries, and the search that establishes the absence stands at pmp:FinnicExactnessSearch."@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 5 a, p. 73, with the order of recitation at pp. 4-5" .

#
# What is said when nothing has happened
#

pmp:FinnicUhkaus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The menacing formula, said after an expulsion that produced nothing"@en, "Die Drohformel, gesprochen nach einer Austreibung, die nichts bewirkt hat"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "uhkasanoja"@en ;
    skos:definition "A formula with its own number and its own occasion in the collection: it is used after an expulsion formula, if the expulsion has proved of no avail."@en ;
    skos:note "The occasion is the point. This is not a stronger version of the expulsion, to be chosen instead of it; it is what is said afterwards, in the case the expulsion was performed and nothing followed. The transmission has given that case a name, a number and a text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 16 a, pp. 92-93, with the occasion stated at p. 3" .

pmc:FinnicUhkauksenPortaat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Six rungs, each on the last having produced nothing"@en, "Sechs Stufen, jede auf dem Ausbleiben der vorigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The construction of the menacing formula, read off its own conditionals. If thou remove not speedily, the speaker will interrogate the addressee's mother. If thou shouldst pay no heed thereto, he will belabour it with willows, rowan shoots and tips of fir. If that be not enough, he will raise a ram with twisted horns and an ox with outspread horns to butt at it. If little should result therefrom, he will take the hands of one deceased from under the ground and claw with them. If that be not enough, he will take the claws of an eagle, then of a bear. And last: now, if it pay no heed thereto, I still remember other means, I know more words of magic power and mightier antagonists — followed by the black dog with a burning mouth that has eaten a hundred men."@en ;
    skos:note "The ladder does not end in a failure. Its last rung is the assertion that further means exist and are known, which is a rung of a different kind from the five below it: those name a thing to be done, this one names a reserve. A practitioner who has said the whole formula and seen nothing has, by the text he has just recited, not run out of procedure."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the same shape this holding carries from the first-century Chinese treatise that puts the regress to a practitioner from outside, and from the nine accounts a run that produced nothing may be assigned to. What is different here is the position: there the regress is described by a critic or inferred from a doctrine, here it is the script, in the second person, in the operative text. A transmission that hands its operator the words for the fifth failure has made the sequence of failures part of the practice rather than an embarrassment to it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 16 a, pp. 92-93" .

pmc:FinnicManauksenPortaat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The same conditional inside an exorcism"@en, "Dieselbe Bedingung innerhalb einer Beschwörung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The construction recurs where the collection does not announce it. An exorcism sends the evil home to its master by nightfall and tells it, on arrival, to kill the mare on the cattle-stand, crush the horse in the stall and the horned ox in the cattle-shed; and then continues: if little should result therefrom, draw the window-shutter back, transfer thyself within, fly into the room like a whirlwind, seize the masters in the farthest nook and the mistresses in the door-corner, break the master's neck and smash the mistress's head."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things follow and both bear on examination. The conditional is a feature of the corpus and not of one formula type, so the menacing formula is a class with a number rather than a peculiarity. And the escalation here is not of the operator's means but of the harm demanded of the evil, directed at the household of whoever sent it — which places the next observable event at a house the operator is not in."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 17 g, pp. 97-98" .

#
# The one deadline and the one sign, and where the sign is to appear
#

pmc:FinnicAurinkoonMennessa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ere the close of day, ere the setting of the sun"@en, "Vor dem Ende des Tages, vor dem Untergang der Sonne"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one term in what was read. The exorcism orders the evil to go, if it has a master, to its master's seat, and if a mistress, to its mistress's feet, to the mouth of its brother's door and the end of its sister's floor — ere the close of day, ere the setting of the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "A term set on the departure and not on the recovery of the patient. Nothing in the passage says what is to be concluded if the sun sets and the sick man is unchanged; the formula's own provision for that case is the conditional two clauses further on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 17 g, p. 97" .

pmc:FinnicMerkkiKotona a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The sign is demanded, and demanded elsewhere"@en, "Das Zeichen wird gefordert, und anderswo gefordert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one sign in what was read. The same exorcism tells the evil that on its arrival at the abode of him that fashioned it, it is to give a sign, a secret signal on reaching home, to cause a crash like a thunder-clap and a glare like a lightning-flash."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered under the separate count SPEC §15 requires for signs of success, and it is the case that shows why the count is kept separate. The sign is perceptible, it is named in advance, and it is to occur at the house of whoever sent the evil — that is, at a place where neither the operator nor the patient is, and at an hour when they are elsewhere. A transmission that names its only confirming event outside the room has stated something no participant could report absent."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the counterpart of the deadline above and belongs beside it: the term is set on the same journey, and the sign is what would mark its completion. Together they are a complete verification procedure with no one at the place of verification."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 17 g, pp. 97-98" .

#
# The state the operator puts himself into first
#

pmp:FinnicLuonnonNostatus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Raising one's own Nature before the work"@en, "Das Erheben der eigenen Natur vor der Arbeit"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "luonnon nostatus"@en, "luonto"@en, "haltia"@en ;
    skos:definition "A formula the operator says over himself and not over the patient. He calls his Nature from its hole and his genius from under a fallen tree, from beneath a stone, from out the moss; he calls his Nature that is hard as stone and his hair that is tough as iron, and the Nature of his mother, his father and his ancestor in addition to his own; he asks to be clothed with a burning coat and with furs of fiery red. The collection names the occasion: it is said for steeling the nerves while conjuring the sick and removing obstacles."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of its lines are the transmission's own account of what the operator brings to the work, and they are inherited rather than acquired: his mother's Nature and his father's and his ancestor's are called up beside his own. That is a prerequisite located in descent, which is not a condition anyone could set, and it stands in the same formula as the one condition anyone could — that the man says these words before he begins."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 12 a-c, pp. 83-84, with the occasion stated at p. 3" .

pmc:FinnicLuontoPresupposesTheWork a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The Nature is raised before the sick are conjured"@en, "Die Natur wird erhoben, ehe der Kranke beschworen wird"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FinnicLuonnonNostatus ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order. The occasion of the formula is stated — while conjuring the sick and removing obstacles — and no consequence is stated for conjuring without it. What raises this above a bare ordering is that the formula is addressed to the operator's own condition and not to the patient's, which makes it the same kind of condition as the intake rules this holding carries from four other transmissions, and in the same direction as most of them: a state to be reached and not one to be avoided."@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 12 a-c, pp. 83-84" .

#
# The onlooker
#

pmc:FinnicKateus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The envious look that spoils the work"@en, "Der neidische Blick, der die Arbeit verdirbt"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the formula against envy names: whoever looks with jealousy, looks pryingly with eyes askew, casts resentful glances or prepares some malevolence — and, in the second variant, whoever keeps turning round with a jealous eye, causes bewitchment with his mouth or imprecates with words. The speaker opens by saying that he cannot guard against envy nor shield himself from opponents while dealing with these toilsome tasks."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction the formula draws is between the envious look and the look without envy, and it is drawn inside the same passage: whoever looks without envy is to be blessed so that he shall have honeyed eyes, and whoever happens to overlook or stares from one side is asked to look with a honeyed glance. The disposition of the onlooker and not his presence is what the formula treats as the variable."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 3 a-c, pp. 70-72" .

pmc:FinnicCautionAgainstTheEnvious a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Against the envious onlooker"@en, "Gegen den neidischen Zuschauer"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:FinnicKateus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    skos:note "The caution is an operative formula and not a rule about who may attend, which is the form this holding has the same claim in from the French magnetic manual. Here there is no instruction to exclude anyone; there is a curse laid on the envious onlooker in advance — may the slag of Hiisi fill his eyes and a fiery bung plug up his mouth — and a blessing on the one who looks without envy. The transmission answers the same danger by acting on the onlooker rather than by removing him."@en ;
    skos:note "The collection adds the exclusion elsewhere and by a different route, in the editor's account of how the work was done rather than in a song: the vapour-bath house was heated as secretly as possible so that no envious or malevolent person should get wind of it and injure the operation. That sentence is the compiler's and is entered at pmc:LonnrotJarjestys, not here."@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 3 a-b, pp. 70-71" .

#
# The order in which all this is recited, which is a compiler's and is held as one
#

pmc:LonnrotJarjestys a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The eighteen classes of formula and the order of recitation"@en, "Die achtzehn Formelklassen und die Reihenfolge des Vortrags"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ordering the collector set over the material and the translator reports from him. The formulae are divided into eighteen classes by occasion, among them the preliminary, the defensive, those against envy, those to discover the cause, the reparation of harm, the expulsion, the posting, the reproaching, the falling into ecstasy, the boasting, the menacing, the exorcism and the making fast. The order of recitation is given as: the vapour formula where there is an open wound, then the preliminary and defensive formulae and those against envy; then, where the injury is of a known kind, the recitation of its origin, and where it is not, the formula to discover the cause; and thereafter the remaining classes in no particular order, as the operator remembered them or thought them useful. The account adds that the work was done in the vapour-bath house, heated as secretly as possible so that no envious person should get wind of it."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Elias Lönnrot, in the preface to Suomen Kansan Muinaisia Loitsurunoja (Helsinki: SKS, 1880), reported and translated by John Abercromby in 1898" ;
    dcterms:date "1880" ;
    skos:note "Held as an ordering act and not as a claim of the transmission, for the reason the registration of this tradition already gave about the Kalevala. The collector states himself that it is hard to say in what order the songs were recited and that if there was an order at all it was not everywhere uniform. An ordering offered with that reservation is exactly what this class is for."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the source of two things this file needs and could not take from a song: that the menacing formula has its occasion after a failed expulsion, and that the discovery formula has its place before the origins. Both are entered above with this node standing behind them, and a later reader who wants the songs alone should subtract them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), pp. 2-6" .
